From e81672fbbdc10b8a78e87b19b65ee29dd394deed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 06:52:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ElementsGeometry: review (no global cache, single ownership,
 tests)

- The gradients of the shape functions of the finite element of the
  transformations are computed for each integration rule, instead of being
  taken from `mfem::FiniteElement::GetDofToQuad`: this finite element is a
  global object, whose cache is indexed by the address of the rule for the
  whole process. Two rules allocated successively at the same address, the
  first one being destroyed, gave wrong jacobians and read out of bounds.
- The jacobians are always owned by the geometric data
  (`ElementsGeometry::jacobians`, a shared pointer). For a mesh with nodes,
  they are computed by `mfem::GeometricFactors` for the geometric data
  instead of being taken from the geometric factors of the mesh, which are
  destroyed when its nodes are modified. The documentation states that the
  data are not updated if the mesh is modified.
- Size check of the vertices of the elements, comment on the release of
  the temporary arrays used by the kernel, costs of the former method
  stated more precisely, `vertices_per_element` instead of `nd` in the
  arguments of the kernel.
- ElementsGeometryTest: mirrored elements (signed determinants), parallel
  meshes refined non conformingly (new variant on two processes), two
  rules allocated successively at the same address, refusal of meshes
  mixing several geometries and of surface meshes.

Mutations detected: global cache of the finite element of the
transformations, absolute value of the determinants, and the six former
ones.
